module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ClosedSubspaceIdealOrderIso
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.RingedToposHomConsequences

/-!
# First-order thickenings of algebraic spaces, and the universal lifting property

An algebraic space `X'` is a *thickening* of an algebraic space `X` when `X` is a closed
subspace of `X'` and the two have the same underlying topological space; that is Stacks 05ZK
part (1), formalized as `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsThickening`.  This file adds the two conditions
that measure the *order* of a thickening by the nilpotence of the defining ideal sheaf of the
closed subspace — Stacks 05ZK parts (4) and (2) — and the universal lifting property of the
diagram of Stacks 0602 which singles out the *universal* first-order thickening of a morphism.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsNthOrderThickening'` (Stacks 05ZK part (4)): the ambient space is
  an `n`th order thickening of a closed subspace of it whose defining ideal has vanishing
  `(n+1)`st power on every object of the small étale site.  This asks only that the subspace be
  closed, where `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsNthOrderThickening` asks it to be a thickening; see
  the implementation notes.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsFirstOrderThickening'` (Stacks 05ZK part (2)): the case `n = 1`,
  the closed subspace being defined by a square-zero ideal.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsUniversalFirstOrderThickening'` (Stacks 0602): a first-order
  thickening `Z ⊆ Z'` over `X` such that every solid diagram of Stacks 0602 — a first-order
  thickening `T ⊆ T'`, a morphism `a : T ⟶ Z` and a morphism `b : T' ⟶ X` over `X` — admits a
  unique dotted arrow `T' ⟶ Z'` restricting to `a` and lying over `X`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_eq_bot_of_retraction`: the defining ideal of a
  closed subspace which the identity of the ambient space factors through is zero on every
  étale chart.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isNthOrderThickening'_top`: an algebraic space is a thickening of
  itself of every order.  This is the inhabitation witness for the two conditions above.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isUniversalFirstOrderThickening'_top`: `X ⊆ X` is a universal
  first-order thickening of `X` over `X`.  This is the inhabitation witness for Stacks 0602,
  and it is the special case of Stacks 0604 in which the morphism is an isomorphism: a
  formally étale morphism is its own universal first-order thickening.

## Implementation notes

**The defining ideal is read off `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt`, not off the order
isomorphism of Stacks 03MB.** Stacks 05ZK speaks of "the quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals
`𝓘 ⊆ 𝒪_{X'}` defining `X`", i.e. of the image of a closed subspace under the correspondence of
Stacks 03MB.  That correspondence is available here only conditionally, as
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.closedSubspaceIdealOrderIso`, whose surjectivity hypothesis `hr` is
unproved — its quasi-coherence hypothesis `hq` *is* proved, unconditionally, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_definingIdealSheaf`.  Its
specification clause however *pins its value*: the ideal it assigns to a closed subspace `Z` is,
on every étale chart `U`, the kernel of the structure map of the inclusion of `Z`, which is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt` by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_eq_ker_fSharp`.  So the conditions below are stated in
terms of `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt` and `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealSheaf`
— both unconditional — and say exactly what Stacks 05ZK says, with no hypothesis carried.

Nothing is lost by not writing the word *quasi-coherent* into the conditions below either.
Stacks 05ZK speaks of "the quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals defining `X`", but quasi-coherence
there is an assertion of Stacks 03MB about the defining ideal sheaf and not a condition imposed
on the closed subspace, and in this library that assertion is proved unconditionally, as
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_definingIdealSheaf`.  So adding it as a conjunct
would change nothing and would only make the conditions harder to use.

The nilpotence equation is read *objectwise*, on each object of the small étale site, rather
than as an equation between sheaves of ideals.  That is legitimate because the sections of the
defining ideal sheaf over a chart are exactly `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt`, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealSheaf_obj`, and because
`SheafOfModules.idealMultiple_le_iff` reduces the vanishing of the subsheaf generated by the
products of sections of two ideal subsheaves to the vanishing of every objectwise product.  The
exponent is chosen before the object, so a single global exponent bounds the order.

The quantifier in `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsNthOrderThickening'` is an `∃ hG`, because the
defining ideal of a subfunctor is only defined once the subfunctor is known to be a closed
subspace.  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealSheaf_obj` makes the chartwise ideal and the
sections of the ideal sheaf the same thing, so nothing is lost by phrasing it chartwise.

**Why the conditions here are primed.** `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsNthOrderThickening` asks
the subspace to be a thickening — closed *and* surjective on points, Stacks 05ZK part (1) —
whereas the condition here asks only that it be closed.  The two agree, because a nilpotent
defining ideal already forces the two spaces of points to coincide, but that implication is not
available in this library (see *What is not proved here* below), so the weaker condition is kept
under the primed name rather than silently identified with the stronger one.
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsFirstOrderThickening'` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsUniversalFirstOrderThickening'` are primed for the same reason: each
is the unprimed condition of the same name with the primed order condition substituted for the
unprimed one, so they too are distinct propositions until that implication is available.

The chartwise reading of `𝓘^{n+1} = 0` agrees with Stacks' sheaf-theoretic equation because
`𝓘^{n+1}` is the subsheaf generated by the presheaf `U ↦ (𝓘 U)^{n+1}`, and a generated subsheaf
of a sheaf vanishes exactly when that generating subpresheaf does.
`Stacks.Sheaves.SheafOfModules.idealMultiple_le_iff` is the one-step case of this, at `N := ⊥`;
the `n`-fold case follows by induction on `n` and is **not** formalized here.

The test thickenings `T ⊆ T'` of `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsUniversalFirstOrderThickening'` range
over all of `AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaces`, which is the type of algebraic spaces over `S`
in this library.  That type is pinned to one `B : BigFppfSite.{u}` and one `S : Scheme.{u}`, so
the test objects are universe- and site-truncated, and the universal property below is formally
*weaker* than the one Stacks states — as is the scheme-level
`Stacks.Thickenings.IsUniversalFirstOrderThickening`, whose implementation notes say the same
thing about itself.  The difference between the two is one of degree, not of kind.

**What is not proved here.** Stacks 0606 — the *existence* of a universal first-order thickening
of a formally unramified morphism — is not proved *in this file*; it is proved in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/Thickenings/UniversalExistence.lean`, which carries the
`@[stacks 0606]` attribute.  Its proof needs the scheme-level existence statement Stacks 04F3,
which *is*
available — `Stacks.Thickenings.nonempty_universalFirstOrderThickening` in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Thickenings/Existence.lean` carries the tag — together with
Stacks 0603 and 0604, and Stacks 0605 *part (2)*, all proved.  Part (2), the descent of the
universal property along a surjective étale morphism of first-order thickenings, is the last
step of the argument of Stacks 0606; it is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsUniversalFirstOrderThickening.of_surjectiveEtale` in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/UniversalFirstOrderThickeningDescent.lean`, beside
part (1), `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsUniversalFirstOrderThickening.etalePullback`.
Likewise the implication from part (4) to part (1), `IsNthOrderThickening'.isThickening`,
is not proved here, and its route is not yet determined: part (1) asserts surjectivity of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.pointsMap`, not a factorization, so
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_comp_eq_iff_forall_ker_le` is not obviously the criterion.  The
pointwise sibling `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.mem_obj_iff_ker_le` is the better candidate, and the
scheme-level analogue in `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Thickenings.lean` instead argues through
the support of the powers of the ideal.

